WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.704 --> 00:00:05.070
IRFAN BAQUI: Hey, everyone.

00:00:05.070 --> 00:00:07.330
Welcome to "Apigee Up Close."

00:00:07.330 --> 00:00:08.370
My name is Irfan Baqui.

00:00:08.370 --> 00:00:10.500
I'm a custom engineer
with Google Cloud,

00:00:10.500 --> 00:00:14.880
helping our customers make
better architectural decisions

00:00:14.880 --> 00:00:17.550
as well as better API
management decisions,

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:22.290
and today we'll be talking about
insights-driven API programs.

00:00:22.290 --> 00:00:25.950
And the objective is just
to share our philosophy

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:30.140
on analytics and monitoring
and relate it to your business.

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:33.570
I'll give you a lot of examples
of how our customers are

00:00:33.570 --> 00:00:36.360
leveraging insights to make
their businesses better,

00:00:36.360 --> 00:00:39.510
to make the API programs
better, and then toward the end

00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:42.420
I'll take you
through a demo that

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:45.960
will showcase everything that we
talk about in the presentation.

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:49.350
But the central idea really
is that a business needs

00:00:49.350 --> 00:00:52.800
real-time insights on multiple
different levels to be agile,

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:57.510
and I'll show you today on how
you can get those insights.

00:00:57.510 --> 00:00:59.400
I'm sure you're
familiar with Apigee.

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:02.850
It sits in the middle
of all of your services

00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:06.420
in your applications, and all
of the data that goes through

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:08.760
between your applications
and your services--

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:10.170
it goes through Apigee.

00:01:10.170 --> 00:01:13.410
And the good thing about
this is that Apigee-- it has

00:01:13.410 --> 00:01:16.240
a lot of data passing
through, and it

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:19.620
can give you the right insights
on how your services are doing,

00:01:19.620 --> 00:01:25.960
both on a business level as
well as on an operational level.

00:01:25.960 --> 00:01:29.830
And when we are talking
about this data,

00:01:29.830 --> 00:01:32.640
the one thing to keep in mind
is that the data is usually

00:01:32.640 --> 00:01:35.238
associated with a
particular entity.

00:01:35.238 --> 00:01:36.780
So on the left you
have the customer.

00:01:36.780 --> 00:01:38.800
On the right you have
your back end systems,

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:41.010
and that's how the data
flows, left to right

00:01:41.010 --> 00:01:42.090
and right to left.

00:01:42.090 --> 00:01:46.590
And it is associated with
a customer, an application,

00:01:46.590 --> 00:01:52.050
and a developer, and it
accesses particular APIs,

00:01:52.050 --> 00:01:55.110
which are part of different
products of yours.

00:01:55.110 --> 00:01:57.600
And in turn, those APIs--

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:02.280
in Apigee, then, they interact
with specific back end systems.

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:03.780
So all of these
insights are very

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:06.310
specific to the
particular entity,

00:02:06.310 --> 00:02:09.449
thus they can give
you a good glean

00:02:09.449 --> 00:02:12.550
on how your business and
your API program is doing.

00:02:12.550 --> 00:02:17.100
So now let me tell you about
our philosophy behind insights.

00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:20.100
We look at insights in a
lot of different levels,

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:22.890
and the first level is
just your core business.

00:02:22.890 --> 00:02:27.210
Everything starts at
your core business KPIs.

00:02:27.210 --> 00:02:30.690
What is your business strategy,
and what are the metrics

00:02:30.690 --> 00:02:32.580
that you need to
track to achieve it?

00:02:32.580 --> 00:02:35.100
And you would want
to track that,

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:37.630
and you can do it in
Apigee in very close

00:02:37.630 --> 00:02:42.750
to a real-time basis, be it your
revenue, or your subscriptions,

00:02:42.750 --> 00:02:45.780
or the health of your
marketing campaigns.

00:02:45.780 --> 00:02:49.690
You can track all of
those insights in Apigee.

00:02:49.690 --> 00:02:53.280
The second one is the insights
about your API program.

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:55.030
How is your API program doing?

00:02:55.030 --> 00:02:57.990
How many developers are signing
up in a particular week?

00:02:57.990 --> 00:03:00.750
The different applications
the developers are making--

00:03:00.750 --> 00:03:02.460
how are they
consuming your APIs?

00:03:02.460 --> 00:03:06.300
What are the experiences that
the end users are getting?

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:09.900
So all of those insights you can
get from a good API management

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:11.190
platform like Apigee.

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:13.660
The next one is at
a product level,

00:03:13.660 --> 00:03:15.360
so within your API
program you would

00:03:15.360 --> 00:03:18.690
be exposing a lot of different
APIs bundled together

00:03:18.690 --> 00:03:22.830
as a product that represent
a cohesive functionality.

00:03:22.830 --> 00:03:25.740
And you want to see how those
products are being consumed

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:29.520
by your customers, and you want
to see how one product relates

00:03:29.520 --> 00:03:33.420
to the other and figure out
how you can make them better,

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:37.190
invest the right resources
to make them better.

00:03:37.190 --> 00:03:39.880
And lastly, the
operational level analytics

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:42.620
for your operations teams, and
these have to be real-time.

00:03:42.620 --> 00:03:47.140
So if there is something wrong
in your APIs on Apigee or even

00:03:47.140 --> 00:03:48.880
in your back end
systems, you want

00:03:48.880 --> 00:03:54.640
to get alerted in real time with
the right amount of information

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:57.820
that will help you solve
that particular error,

00:03:57.820 --> 00:04:00.820
and you'd be able to
get that with Apigee.

00:04:00.820 --> 00:04:03.550
So now let me talk about
some of our customers that

00:04:03.550 --> 00:04:06.980
are using Apigee Analytics on
all of these different levels.

00:04:06.980 --> 00:04:09.580
So one of the customers I want
to talk about is T-Mobile.

00:04:09.580 --> 00:04:13.000
T-Mobile is one of the largest
telecommunications providers

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:16.779
in the United States, and they
are reinventing themselves

00:04:16.779 --> 00:04:18.490
as a technology company.

00:04:18.490 --> 00:04:20.990
They are modernizing
their entire stack,

00:04:20.990 --> 00:04:23.410
and they're using
Apigee as the API layer

00:04:23.410 --> 00:04:26.980
on top of everything to manage
the APIs, and govern them,

00:04:26.980 --> 00:04:27.980
and all of that.

00:04:27.980 --> 00:04:31.330
So one of their key business
metrics that they track

00:04:31.330 --> 00:04:33.310
are subscriptions.

00:04:33.310 --> 00:04:36.640
And they're able to
get business insights

00:04:36.640 --> 00:04:41.170
into how the API program is
supporting that key business

00:04:41.170 --> 00:04:44.020
strategy and how many
subscriptions they're driving.

00:04:44.020 --> 00:04:47.720
So thinking about T-Mobile,
it has a good online presence.

00:04:47.720 --> 00:04:50.560
It also has a very
good brick-and-mortar,

00:04:50.560 --> 00:04:53.440
concrete, offline presence.

00:04:53.440 --> 00:04:56.830
It has thousands of
stores across the US,

00:04:56.830 --> 00:04:58.960
but at the same time,
they're also selling

00:04:58.960 --> 00:05:01.090
through big-box retailers.

00:05:01.090 --> 00:05:07.870
And they would want to find
out what kinds of demographics

00:05:07.870 --> 00:05:12.460
resonate better in one
channel versus the other.

00:05:12.460 --> 00:05:16.150
The people who would buy
T-Mobile subscriptions

00:05:16.150 --> 00:05:20.710
in the retailer Target may
be of a different demographic

00:05:20.710 --> 00:05:22.930
than folks that buy
the subscriptions

00:05:22.930 --> 00:05:25.490
at Best Buy or Walmart.

00:05:25.490 --> 00:05:28.990
And you want to make sure that
you are appealing to them,

00:05:28.990 --> 00:05:31.840
you're providing the right kind
of product to each one of them.

00:05:31.840 --> 00:05:35.080
And that's possible by
gaining the right insights

00:05:35.080 --> 00:05:36.520
from that demographic.

00:05:36.520 --> 00:05:40.330
The other customer I want to
talk about is Domino's Pizza.

00:05:40.330 --> 00:05:42.670
Now, you would think that
Domino's is in the pizza

00:05:42.670 --> 00:05:44.560
delivery business,
but actually they

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:48.700
are in a B2B business, servicing
their franchise owners,

00:05:48.700 --> 00:05:50.540
and these franchisees--

00:05:50.540 --> 00:05:52.540
they have their own IT systems.

00:05:52.540 --> 00:05:57.460
Then Domino's needs a way to
integrate into their delivery

00:05:57.460 --> 00:05:59.770
systems, and their payment
systems, and all of that,

00:05:59.770 --> 00:06:02.720
and they are able to
do that with Apigee.

00:06:02.720 --> 00:06:08.050
But at the same time, to
stay competitive and lure

00:06:08.050 --> 00:06:12.760
the franchise owners in staying
with Domino's and opening up

00:06:12.760 --> 00:06:16.690
even more franchises,
they need to provide them

00:06:16.690 --> 00:06:18.880
with relevant
insights on how they

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:21.250
can make their programs better.

00:06:21.250 --> 00:06:24.610
And with a platform like
Apigee, as all of the data

00:06:24.610 --> 00:06:26.530
is flowing through,
they would be

00:06:26.530 --> 00:06:32.500
able to give them insights on
what does the best delivery

00:06:32.500 --> 00:06:34.930
experience look like?

00:06:34.930 --> 00:06:37.000
Who are the target
demographics, what

00:06:37.000 --> 00:06:40.810
are they interested in
across different geographies,

00:06:40.810 --> 00:06:44.470
and what are the success
factors and success criterias

00:06:44.470 --> 00:06:47.890
that the franchise owners
can implement to make

00:06:47.890 --> 00:06:49.660
their businesses better?

00:06:49.660 --> 00:06:52.570
The other customer I want to
talk to you about is Walgreens,

00:06:52.570 --> 00:06:55.060
and Walgreens is really
a classic example

00:06:55.060 --> 00:07:01.600
of a customer that has developed
a robust developer ecosystem.

00:07:01.600 --> 00:07:05.590
Walgreens-- if you're aware, it
is one of the largest pharmacy

00:07:05.590 --> 00:07:07.270
chains in the United States.

00:07:07.270 --> 00:07:11.620
Back in 2008, they started
to experience a drop

00:07:11.620 --> 00:07:14.380
in their photo business because
folks weren't using film

00:07:14.380 --> 00:07:18.070
rolls anymore, and they were
using their smartphones instead

00:07:18.070 --> 00:07:19.400
to take pictures.

00:07:19.400 --> 00:07:22.300
And then it was really
important for them

00:07:22.300 --> 00:07:24.940
to understand the
end user behaviors

00:07:24.940 --> 00:07:28.610
and figure out how to
get those users back.

00:07:28.610 --> 00:07:31.220
Well, they found out that
they were taking pictures

00:07:31.220 --> 00:07:33.620
with their smartphones,
but they still

00:07:33.620 --> 00:07:36.920
wanted to get those
pictures printed.

00:07:36.920 --> 00:07:40.130
So they created some native
applications, mobile as well as

00:07:40.130 --> 00:07:43.700
desktop, to
basically enable them

00:07:43.700 --> 00:07:47.210
to get those photos printed
at a nearby Walgreens.

00:07:47.210 --> 00:07:49.860
And that restored some
of their business,

00:07:49.860 --> 00:07:52.040
but they said,
hey, I think there

00:07:52.040 --> 00:07:54.060
is some more potential here.

00:07:54.060 --> 00:07:58.970
And they started to get insights
into what kinds of pictures

00:07:58.970 --> 00:08:01.790
were these customers printing.

00:08:01.790 --> 00:08:04.640
And they found out that
a lot of this traffic

00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:10.100
was coming from other photo
services that would enhance

00:08:10.100 --> 00:08:11.340
their customers' photos.

00:08:11.340 --> 00:08:14.240
For example, at
Halloween time you

00:08:14.240 --> 00:08:17.450
would upload a picture
to one of these services

00:08:17.450 --> 00:08:20.480
and decorate it with
pumpkins or something.

00:08:20.480 --> 00:08:24.250
So they were experiencing a lot
of traffic from such services.

00:08:24.250 --> 00:08:29.240
They said, all right, how
about I expose my photo prints

00:08:29.240 --> 00:08:32.690
API to these different
applications and services

00:08:32.690 --> 00:08:36.799
and incentivize them
to route their traffic

00:08:36.799 --> 00:08:40.100
to get the photos printed
at a nearby Walgreens store?

00:08:40.100 --> 00:08:44.630
So they did that, and
their ecosystem exploded.

00:08:44.630 --> 00:08:47.180
There were actually net new
apps that the developers

00:08:47.180 --> 00:08:51.050
were making just to
tap into the incentives

00:08:51.050 --> 00:08:55.220
that Walgreens would give
them to direct traffic

00:08:55.220 --> 00:08:56.400
to their stores.

00:08:56.400 --> 00:08:57.740
So that was very successful.

00:08:57.740 --> 00:09:02.060
And then they replicated
that model to other products

00:09:02.060 --> 00:09:03.090
as well.

00:09:03.090 --> 00:09:06.950
So now they have a plethora
of products ranging

00:09:06.950 --> 00:09:10.580
from prescription refills,
and rewards programs, and home

00:09:10.580 --> 00:09:14.870
delivery services that
are basically just

00:09:14.870 --> 00:09:16.490
APIs that they're exposing.

00:09:16.490 --> 00:09:20.240
And they're being integrated
in the developer's applications

00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:25.850
and thereby giving more business
and traffic to Walgreens.

00:09:25.850 --> 00:09:29.240
And they were able to do
so by really understanding

00:09:29.240 --> 00:09:32.240
their end customers and
their shopping patterns

00:09:32.240 --> 00:09:36.050
as well as understanding
developers, what they need,

00:09:36.050 --> 00:09:40.850
and incentivizing them
to partner with them

00:09:40.850 --> 00:09:42.812
and send them the right traffic.

00:09:42.812 --> 00:09:44.270
The next thing I
want to talk about

00:09:44.270 --> 00:09:46.490
is operational insights
and monitoring,

00:09:46.490 --> 00:09:48.970
and this is actually used
by a lot of our customers

00:09:48.970 --> 00:09:50.345
and a lot of
different verticals.

00:09:50.345 --> 00:09:54.050
So I won't to give you a
particular customer's example.

00:09:54.050 --> 00:09:57.920
But let me tell you about
the motivation, actually,

00:09:57.920 --> 00:10:05.060
of why we created the
Monitoring module in Apigee.

00:10:05.060 --> 00:10:09.230
Some of the largest retailers
in the US and across the globe,

00:10:09.230 --> 00:10:12.080
actually, are
Apigee's customers.

00:10:12.080 --> 00:10:15.110
And at critical
times of the year,

00:10:15.110 --> 00:10:19.430
like the New Year's sale or the
Black Friday or Cyber Monday

00:10:19.430 --> 00:10:24.010
sale, they experience
enormous amount of traffic.

00:10:24.010 --> 00:10:27.620
So for example, in
2018, Black Friday,

00:10:27.620 --> 00:10:31.910
we experienced close
to 110,000 transactions

00:10:31.910 --> 00:10:35.570
per second sustained over
long durations of time.

00:10:35.570 --> 00:10:40.280
And it's important that these
services for these customers--

00:10:40.280 --> 00:10:42.860
they're up and running
without any disruption

00:10:42.860 --> 00:10:45.740
because every
second of disruption

00:10:45.740 --> 00:10:49.340
could cost them hundreds of
thousands, if not millions,

00:10:49.340 --> 00:10:52.070
of dollars in revenue.

00:10:52.070 --> 00:10:56.960
So Apigee has a really
robust API availability rate.

00:10:56.960 --> 00:10:58.580
We surpass our SLAs.

00:10:58.580 --> 00:11:00.080
But our customers
said that, hey,

00:11:00.080 --> 00:11:03.740
sometimes there are
things on our end

00:11:03.740 --> 00:11:05.030
that we need to troubleshoot.

00:11:05.030 --> 00:11:08.840
Some of our services
get down for a variety

00:11:08.840 --> 00:11:10.400
of different
reasons, and we would

00:11:10.400 --> 00:11:14.720
love to get some
alerts for us to be

00:11:14.720 --> 00:11:17.690
able to fix those
issues fast and get up

00:11:17.690 --> 00:11:18.800
and running really fast.

00:11:18.800 --> 00:11:22.220
So that was the motivation
behind Apigee Monitoring.

00:11:22.220 --> 00:11:27.125
It addresses a particular
need for the operations teams.

00:11:27.125 --> 00:11:29.060
And there's a lot of
pressure these days

00:11:29.060 --> 00:11:32.660
for them to resolve issues very
fast, quickly diagnose issues,

00:11:32.660 --> 00:11:35.930
and the data that
Apigee has, which

00:11:35.930 --> 00:11:39.680
is specific to the
entities accessing the APIs

00:11:39.680 --> 00:11:43.310
as well as services
that the API is tied to.

00:11:43.310 --> 00:11:46.610
And the different
policies in your proxies

00:11:46.610 --> 00:11:50.330
and where exactly that
error might be occurring

00:11:50.330 --> 00:11:55.530
is very useful in diagnosing
these APIs issues.

00:11:55.530 --> 00:11:59.150
So this is a diagram of how
Apigee monitoring differs

00:11:59.150 --> 00:12:01.160
from just generic
synthetic monitoring.

00:12:01.160 --> 00:12:04.670
The thing I want
you to focus on is

00:12:04.670 --> 00:12:07.280
that the alerts that you get--

00:12:07.280 --> 00:12:08.210
they are precise.

00:12:08.210 --> 00:12:11.870
They tell you exactly where
the error has occurred.

00:12:11.870 --> 00:12:13.040
They're contextual.

00:12:13.040 --> 00:12:16.460
If the error is due
to a raised condition,

00:12:16.460 --> 00:12:18.920
if it's intermittent,
it'll help you

00:12:18.920 --> 00:12:24.140
zero-in on what exactly is
wrong and what is triggering

00:12:24.140 --> 00:12:25.680
that particular error.

00:12:25.680 --> 00:12:29.060
So just to sum it up,
Apigee Analytics actually

00:12:29.060 --> 00:12:35.000
covers all of the personas
that need insights into the API

00:12:35.000 --> 00:12:37.730
program to make it better.

00:12:37.730 --> 00:12:40.040
It is your business
owner to get insights

00:12:40.040 --> 00:12:42.530
on the strategy
for the API program

00:12:42.530 --> 00:12:45.680
and how it's doing with
relation to the different KPIs

00:12:45.680 --> 00:12:47.460
that the business team has set--

00:12:47.460 --> 00:12:50.990
so the API team to monitor
the health of the API program

00:12:50.990 --> 00:12:54.290
and figure out what
is gaining traction,

00:12:54.290 --> 00:12:56.540
what needs more resources--

00:12:56.540 --> 00:12:59.570
to the app developer to gain
insights into particular API

00:12:59.570 --> 00:13:04.400
proxies and applications,
and to your ops teams

00:13:04.400 --> 00:13:07.190
to get real-time
monitoring alerts

00:13:07.190 --> 00:13:10.010
and help in
diagnosing the issues.

00:13:10.010 --> 00:13:13.000
Let me jump right into a demo.

00:13:13.000 --> 00:13:16.010
So this is how the
Apigee platform looks.

00:13:16.010 --> 00:13:18.670
You have a place to
design your APIs.

00:13:18.670 --> 00:13:21.220
You have a place to
create, build your APIs,

00:13:21.220 --> 00:13:23.600
productize them, publish
them, and all of that,

00:13:23.600 --> 00:13:26.560
and then you have a
place for analytics.

00:13:26.560 --> 00:13:29.320
And to the left of the
screen, as you can see,

00:13:29.320 --> 00:13:33.400
these are the out-of-the-box
dashboards that you have

00:13:33.400 --> 00:13:38.480
to target specific personas
within your organization.

00:13:38.480 --> 00:13:42.220
I would like to
begin with developers

00:13:42.220 --> 00:13:43.965
because once you
have an API program,

00:13:43.965 --> 00:13:45.340
usually the first
thing you think

00:13:45.340 --> 00:13:49.420
about is, how are my
developers consuming

00:13:49.420 --> 00:13:52.280
my APIs, what is my engagement
rate, and all of that?

00:13:52.280 --> 00:13:56.780
So this dashboard will
give us just that.

00:13:56.780 --> 00:14:00.500
So right now, as
you can see, there

00:14:00.500 --> 00:14:02.810
is a funnel on the
screen, and you'll

00:14:02.810 --> 00:14:05.360
be able to see how many
developers have signed up

